<p>I only meant to see whether the new Epic Games Launcher would open on Cthulhu. A few minutes later I was standing outside the castle in <em>Castlevania</em>, with the old NES tune stuck in my head again. Then I downloaded Farming Simulator 22 to try a bigger 3D game. <strong>Both ran in my test on Arch Linux with dwm on X11.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Updated 13 November 2026:</strong> Epic plans Fortnite on Linux with EAC v2. The <a href="#fortnite">new Fortnite section</a> covers TPM 2.0, Secure Boot and CachyOS preparation.</p>

<h2 id="arch-installation">Installing the Epic Games Launcher on Arch Linux</h2>
<p>On 10 November 2026 I ran <strong>Epic Games Launcher Preview 0.113.1</strong>. I installed version <code>0.113.1-1</code> of <code>epic-games-launcher-bin</code> from the AUR. The community package takes Epic’s Linux download and packages it for Arch. Epic’s official Linux requirements do not list Arch.</p>
<pre><code>yay -S epic-games-launcher-bin</code></pre>
<p>Review the AUR <code>PKGBUILD</code> when prompted. Once installed, I launched it from a terminal as my normal user.</p>
<pre><code>epic-games-launcher</code></pre>
<p>The launcher opened and the library loaded after I signed in. Cthulhu has a Ryzen 7 5800X3D, a 16 GB Radeon RX 9060 XT, and dwm on X11. My <a href="/blog/arch-linux-dwm-cthulhu/">Arch and dwm setup</a> has its own write-up.</p>
<figure class="article-figure"><a href="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/launcher-library.webp"><img src="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/launcher-library.webp" width="2004" height="878" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Epic library favourites with seven game covers, including Castlevania, Farming Simulator 22 and Fortnite."></a><figcaption>My Epic library favourites after signing in on Arch Linux.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 id="wine-proton">Which Wine or Proton version does Epic use?</h2>
<p>On the first click of <em>Play</em> for <strong>Castlevania Anniversary Collection</strong>, the launcher offered a one-time setup for Windows games. I accepted and it downloaded <strong>493 MB</strong> of extra components in this test. I could leave runner selection and Wine prefix setup to the launcher. The dialog said that subsequent Windows games would use the same setup.</p>
<div class="epic-setup-images">
<figure class="article-figure"><a href="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/windows-setup.webp"><img src="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/windows-setup.webp" width="577" height="325" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="One-time Windows game setup dialog with a German Install and Play button."></a><figcaption>The launcher offers one-time setup on the first game launch.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="article-figure"><a href="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/windows-setup-download.webp"><img src="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/windows-setup-download.webp" width="557" height="386" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="One-time setup progress bar at 50 MB out of 493 MB."></a><figcaption>The launcher downloaded 493 MB of extra components in this test.</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<p>Epic’s release notes document the launcher version. I couldn’t find an exact Wine or Proton build listed there. GamingOnLinux had already found GE-Proton in the Preview, so I read the version file in the launcher’s runtime directory on Cthulhu. This command also works in fish and uses the default path under <code>~/.local/share</code>.</p>
<pre><code>find "$HOME/.local/share/com.epicgames.eglv2/compat/runtime" -maxdepth 4 -type f -name version -print -exec head -n 2 {} \;</code></pre>
<pre><code>/home/nebu/.local/share/com.epicgames.eglv2/compat/runtime/GE-Proton10-34/version
1774238111 GE-Proton10-34</code></pre>
<p><strong>GE-Proton10-34</strong> was the runtime I found in Epic’s directory for this test. The later <code>top</code> screenshot also shows active Wine processes. I didn’t trace each component build separately for each game. The part I noticed while playing was simpler. I could click Play in the launcher.</p>

<h2 id="castlevania">Castlevania Anniversary Collection on Arch Linux</h2>
<p>The collection launched after setup. I opened the original <em>Castlevania</em> and played Stage 01. By the time I was outside the castle, the tune was already back in my head. The music is one of the things I remember most about playing it on the NES. A quick launch test had turned into a return to a game from my <a href="/blog/my-gaming-journey-1984-2026/">gaming journey</a>.</p>
<figure class="article-figure"><a href="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/castlevania-gameplay.webp"><img src="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/castlevania-gameplay.webp" width="1912" height="1076" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="The original Castlevania in Stage 01 with the player character, candles and health bars."></a><figcaption>Castlevania Anniversary Collection on Cthulhu, playing Stage 01.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 id="farming-simulator-22">Farming Simulator 22 on Arch Linux</h2>
<p>I know nothing about farming. When my neighbour drove his tractor out into the field, I’d look over the top of my monitor to see what he was doing, then try it in Farming Simulator. The rest of my agricultural education comes from <em>Clarkson’s Farm</em>. I laughed enough at <em>Top Gear</em> and <em>The Grand Tour</em>. Apparently a tractor will do it now.</p>
<p>I liked the download screen. It separates network and disk write speeds and puts free space and the install location alongside them. For FS22 the screenshot shows a <strong>25.8 GB download</strong> and <strong>38.1 GB to write</strong>. Having both figures makes it easier to see what the install is going to need.</p>
<figure class="article-figure"><a href="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/launcher-downloads.webp"><img src="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/launcher-downloads.webp" width="2048" height="925" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Epic download view with Farming Simulator 22 in progress and separate network, write and free-space indicators."></a><figcaption>FS22 is downloading. Network speed, write speed and available space are on the right.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Farming Simulator 22</strong> launched and ran smoothly into the playable 3D world in my test. The screenshot shows me on the farm with the HUD, minimap and interaction markers visible. The numbers below come from a single <code>top</code> sample taken during the session.</p>
<figure class="article-figure"><a href="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/farming-simulator-22-gameplay.webp"><img src="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/farming-simulator-22-gameplay.webp" width="2048" height="833" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Farming Simulator 22 gameplay showing a farm, cows, mountains and the in-game HUD."></a><figcaption>FS22 on Arch Linux and dwm. The 3D world has loaded and is playable.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="article-figure"><a href="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/farming-simulator-22-top.webp"><img src="/assets/blog/epic-games-launcher-linux/farming-simulator-22-top.webp" width="927" height="496" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Terminal showing top during the FS22 test, with the simulator, wineserver, winedevice.exe, Xorg and the Epic launcher in the process list."></a><figcaption>Original top screenshot taken during the session on Cthulhu.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The list shows the simulator, <code>wineserver</code>, <code>winedevice.exe</code>, <code>Xorg</code> and the Epic launcher. The simulator shows about <strong>1.9 GiB of resident memory</strong> (<code>RES</code>). The <strong>85.8%</strong> is in the game process’s CPU column. The separate system summary shows <strong>92.7% CPU idle</strong>. This is a snapshot of CPU and RAM. I didn’t measure FPS, GPU load or long-session stability here.</p>

<h2 id="fortnite">Can you play Fortnite on Linux?</h2>
<p><strong>As of 13 November 2026, Fortnite is not officially playable on Linux.</strong> It appears in my Epic library, but that does not mean it launches. Epic plans to <strong>pilot Easy Anti-Cheat Linux v2 in Fortnite first</strong>; no release date has been confirmed. See <a href="https://www.epicgames.com/help/c-34254770/c-33726977/can-i-play-fortnite-on-linux-a22610900">Epic's Fortnite Linux FAQ</a> and the <a href="https://onlineservices.epicgames.com/news/introducing-easy-anti-cheat-linux-v2">official announcement from 14 October 2026</a>.</p>
<h2 id="fortnite-eac">Easy Anti-Cheat Linux v2: TPM 2.0 and Secure Boot are only the beginning</h2>
<p>Epic requires <strong>TPM 2.0</strong> (not “TPM 3”), <strong>Secure Boot</strong>, a kernel signed with recognized keys and appropriately signed kernel modules. Its list also includes <strong>measured boot</strong>, an enabled <strong>IOMMU</strong> without passthrough on ordinary PCs, <strong>kernel lockdown in integrity mode</strong>, disabled <code>kexec</code> and an enabled <strong>BPF LSM</strong>. Just flipping the Secure Boot switch will not be enough. Custom kernels and unsigned out-of-tree modules may be challenging.</p>
<p>Unlike on Windows, Epic says EAC Linux v2 will <strong>not use its own anti-cheat kernel module</strong>. It will instead use Linux security features, eBPF and a <code>systemd</code> service that runs only while a protected game is running. The requirements may change before launch. Working today with older EAC support under Proton does not automatically establish EAC v2 compatibility.</p>
<h2 id="fortnite-distros">Which Linux distributions are ready for Fortnite and EAC v2?</h2>
<p>Gaming performance and anti-cheat compatibility are not the same. In the <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/hwsurvey/?platform=linux">October 2026 Steam Hardware Survey (Linux only)</a>, CachyOS leads the individually listed distributions at <strong>16.39%</strong>. Selected other entries are Arch Linux (8.92%), Linux Mint 22.3 (8.41%), Bazzite (6.74%), Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS (3.63%) and Debian 13 (1.88%). This voluntary Steam survey is <strong>not a count of all Linux users</strong>, and it does not establish their ages or whether they recently migrated from Windows.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Distro comparison for Secure Boot and Fortnite EAC v2" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Distribution</th><th scope="col">Secure Boot / keys</th><th scope="col">Fortnite with EAC v2?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Ubuntu and Linux Mint</th><td>Canonical key recognized by Epic; signed default boot chain available.</td><td>Good basis, but not automatically approved.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Debian</th><td>Debian Secure Boot CA on Epic's list.</td><td>Promising test candidate; configuration matters.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Fedora and Silverblue</th><td>Fedora Secure Boot CA on Epic's list.</td><td>Recognized signing path, no launch guarantee.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Universal Blue: Bazzite, Bluefin, Aurora</th><td>Universal Blue key explicitly listed by Epic.</td><td>Interesting basis for later testing.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">openSUSE</th><td>openSUSE Secure Boot CA listed by Epic.</td><td>Signing basis available; other checks still apply.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Arch Linux and EndeavourOS</th><td>Secure Boot can be configured, but the default kernel is not automatically recognized by Epic.</td><td>Not ready without additional changes.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">CachyOS</th><td>Dedicated signed EAC kernel and separate boot path are documented.</td><td>Ordinary install is not automatically compatible; Epic recognition remains open.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>This compares <strong>preparedness, not proven Fortnite compatibility</strong>. Epic lists trusted <em>signing keys</em>, not certified distributions. TPM 2.0 is a hardware/firmware feature, not something the distro simply ships. Secure Boot state, loaded modules and kernel settings must be evaluated on each machine.</p>
<h2 id="cachyos-eac">CachyOS is already preparing for EAC Linux v2</h2>
<p>CachyOS is popular for Linux gaming, but an ordinary installation is <strong>not automatically compatible with Fortnite's planned EAC v2 checks</strong>. Its developers have already acted: the <a href="https://wiki.cachyos.org/configuration/easy_anti_cheat/">official CachyOS Easy Anti-Cheat Linux v2 guide</a> documents a specially signed <code>linux-cachyos-eac</code> kernel, matching signed <code>systemd-boot</code> and a <strong>separate EAC boot entry</strong>. Normal boot options remain in place.</p>
<p><code>sudo eac-setup</code> prepares this route, checks TPM and IOMMU, enrolls keys and installs matching packages. Firmware settings remain the user's responsibility. <code>sudo eac-setup boot</code> selects the EAC environment for <em>one reboot</em>; <code>sudo eac-status</code> checks local setup. For Limine and rEFInd, the wiki requires the specially signed systemd-boot route. Booting the EAC kernel directly from Limine fails the checks, and arbitrary DKMS modules cannot be loaded in the restricted EAC boot.</p>
<p><strong>This is concrete preparation, not proof Fortnite works.</strong> The CachyOS EAC CA is not yet explicitly listed in Epic's published recognized-key list. Whether Epic ultimately trusts this route and Fortnite launches through it remains to be seen. A clean <code>eac-status</code> check is not enough. The <a href="https://wiki.cachyos.org/configuration/easy_anti_cheat/">official CachyOS wiki</a> has complete setup instructions and warnings about Secure Boot key changes, BitLocker and TPM-bound LUKS.</p>
<h2 id="fortnite-testplan">My test plan: Debian, Ubuntu and Universal Blue on a separate M.2 SSD</h2>
<p>My daily machine stays on <strong>Arch Linux with dwm on Cthulhu</strong>. I will not rebuild it for one game. Once Fortnite officially reaches Linux, I plan to disconnect my existing drives and test fresh installations of <strong>Debian, Ubuntu and Universal Blue</strong> one after another on a <strong>separate M.2 SSD</strong>. Then my regular Arch setup goes back in. These are <strong>future tests, not results</strong>.</p>
<p>Removing disks protects my everyday installations from accidental drive changes, but it does <strong>not isolate global UEFI, Secure Boot and TPM settings</strong>. I will document those before the tests and restore the appropriate configuration afterward. Once tested, I will publish actual outcomes and error messages.</p>

